# cargo-tauri 2.12.2 — corresponding source

This document records where the source corresponding to the packaged Tauri CLI
candidate came from, and how each architecture's binary was produced. It is a
factual record of one candidate build, not a statement that the corresponding
source obligation is satisfied: the arm64 package was compiled from the upstream
sources named below, and the package carries the sources' licence texts.

## What each architecture contains

| Architecture | Binary | Provenance |
|---|---|---|
| `amd64` | `/usr/bin/cargo-tauri` | the official upstream release asset for `tauri-cli-v2.12.2`, downloaded and verified against its SHA-256 digest |
| `arm64` | `/usr/bin/cargo-tauri` | compiled from the source below by the native ARM CI job; there is no official Linux arm64 release asset |

The two packages are built from the same upstream version and tag, but only the
amd64 one is an official upstream binary. The arm64 package is a source build and
states so in `build-provenance.json`; the amd64 package states its asset digest
in `official-asset.json`.

## Corresponding upstream source

| Item | Value |
|---|---|
| Release tag | `tauri-cli-v2.12.2` |
| Source commit | `5b23c82ea5ba6b9aec420dd71f60493edc486aa9` |
| Crate manifest | `crates/tauri-cli/Cargo.toml` |
| `Cargo.lock` git blob | `6b052c58398b8d6ec246d77f65a748448cd4c859` |
| Rust toolchain | `1.99.0` |
| Target triple | `aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu` |
| Cargo profile | `release-size-optimized` |

Source tree:
<https://github.com/tauri-apps/tauri/tree/tauri-cli-v2.12.2>

Every reference above is pinned to an exact tag or commit, never to `main` or to
a branch head. The build runs `cargo build --locked`, so the lockfile at that
commit is the one the compiler used; its git object id is recorded above, and the
SHA-256 of the exact bytes the build read is recorded in `build-provenance.json`.

## Rebuilding from source

The arm64 package is compiled on a native ARM64 Linux machine from that pinned
commit, with the reviewed command:

```sh
cargo build --locked --manifest-path crates/tauri-cli/Cargo.toml --target aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu --profile release-size-optimized --bin cargo-tauri
```

The compiler is the Rust `1.99.0` stable toolchain, whose `rustc -Vv` and
`cargo -V` output is recorded in `build-provenance.json`. Dependencies are
resolved from crates.io against the upstream lockfile; nothing is vendored,
mirrored, or built offline, and no private registry or mirror is configured.

The build needs a C toolchain and the GTK and OpenSSL development headers that
upstream's build scripts probe for. Those are build-time tools on the ephemeral
CI machine: the installed package depends only on the libraries the finished
binary actually links, which `build-provenance.json` and the package's `Depends`
field state.

Upstream ships this crate as `tauri-cli` with the binary named `cargo-tauri`, so
`cargo tauri ...` invokes it as a Cargo external subcommand.

## Official release asset

The amd64 package's binary is the official upstream release asset:

<https://github.com/tauri-apps/tauri/releases/download/tauri-cli-v2.12.2/cargo-tauri-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tgz>

Its SHA-256 digest is
`35c7b50266256cb6d5f757608d7097a59a5faeb0c75879ee1f504652a9b6087e`. The two
licence files installed here are the `LICENSE-MIT` and `LICENSE-APACHE-2.0`
members of that asset, byte for byte identical to the same files at the commit
above.

## Licensing

Upstream is dual licensed: `MIT OR Apache-2.0`. Both licence texts are installed
in this package as `LICENSE-MIT.txt` and `LICENSE-APACHE-2.0.txt`, and both are
quoted in the `copyright` file.
